[[:db/cas 17592186629344 :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -4 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & 1 & -3 \\\\  & 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}=x-3+\\frac{4}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x-3+\\frac{4}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -4 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & 1 & -3 \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & -3 & 4\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}=x-3+\\frac{4}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+7}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x-3+\\frac{4}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186630938 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(G),$ we need to check if the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[G\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[G\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -7 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-2-2{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nand from the first one, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1-2(-2-2{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=5+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(G).$ Since we are interested in writing $b$ as $b={x}_{1}{g}_{1}+{x}_{2}{g}_{2}$, that is to say, as a linear combination of the first two columns, then we need to set ${x}_{3}=0.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=5\\cdot (-2)=-10.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(G),$ we need to check if the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[G\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[G\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -7 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-2-2{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nand from the first one, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1-2(-2-2{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=5+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $Gx=b$ is consistent which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(G).$ Since we are interested in writing $b$ as $b={x}_{1}{g}_{1}+{x}_{2}{g}_{2}$, that is to say, as a linear combination of the first two columns, then we need to set ${x}_{3}=0.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{g}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=5\\cdot (-2)=-10.$"] [:db/cas 17592186630980 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=-6 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the  third equation, we get a false statement $0=10.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -7 & -2 & -1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=-6 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the  third equation, we get a false statement $0=10.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"] [:db/cas 17592186630988 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(H),$ then the equation $Hx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[H\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[H\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6k+7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3k+6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=k+1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3k-1 \\\\ 0 &=3k+6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=3k+6,$ which means that $k=-2.$" "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(H),$ then the equation $Hx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[H\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[H\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6k+7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & k+1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3k-1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3k+6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=k+1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3k-1 \\\\ 0 &=3k+6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=3k+6,$ which means that $k=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186631860 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $8,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $-8.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{8v}-9w & =-7 \\\\ -\\cancel{8v}+11w & =5 \\\\ (-9w+11w) & =(-7+5) \\\\ 2w & =-2 \\\\ w & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=-1.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8v-9w & =-7 \\\\ 8v-9(-1) & =-7 \\\\ 8v+9 & =-7 \\\\ 8v & =-16 \\\\ v & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=-2,\\,w=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,-1).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $8,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $-8.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{8v}-9w & =-7 \\\\ -\\cancel{8v}+11w & =5 \\\\ \\hline (-9w+11w) & =(-7+5) \\\\ 2w & =-2 \\\\ w & =-1\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=-1.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8v-9w & =-7 \\\\ 8v-9(-1) & =-7 \\\\ 8v+9 & =-7 \\\\ 8v & =-16 \\\\ v & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=-2,\\,w=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631882 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-4,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $4.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cancel{-4y} & =4 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{4y} & =4 \\\\ (x+3x) & =(4+4) \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4y & =4 \\\\ 2-4y & =4 \\\\ -4y & =2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-4,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $4.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x\\cancel{-4y} & =4 \\\\ 3x+\\cancel{4y} & =4 \\\\ \\hline (x+3x) & =(4+4) \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4y & =4 \\\\ 2-4y & =4 \\\\ -4y & =2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631883 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=19 \\\\ (\\,-x+4y=10\\,)\\,\\times (2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y &=19 \\\\ -2x+8y &=20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+5y & =19 \\\\ -\\cancel{2x}+8y & =20 \\\\ (5y+8y) & =(19+20) \\\\ 13y & =39 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =10 \\\\ -x+4(3) & =10 \\\\ -x+12 & =10 \\\\ -x & =-2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(2,3).$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=19 \\\\ (\\,-x+4y=10\\,)\\,\\times (2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+5y &=19 \\\\ -2x+8y &=20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2x}+5y & =19 \\\\ -\\cancel{2x}+8y & =20 \\\\ \\hline (5y+8y) & =(19+20) \\\\ 13y & =39 \\\\ y & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =10 \\\\ -x+4(3) & =10 \\\\ -x+12 & =10 \\\\ -x & =-2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(2,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631894 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $2,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $-2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2v}-5w & =5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2v}+11w & =1 \\\\ (-5w+11w) & =(5+1) \\\\ 6w & =6 \\\\ w & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=1.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-5w & =5 \\\\ 2v-5(1) & =5 \\\\ 2v-5 & =5 \\\\ 2v & =10 \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=5,\\,w=1,$ which can be expressed as $(5,1).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $2,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $-2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2v}-5w & =5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2v}+11w & =1 \\\\ \\hline (-5w+11w) & =(5+1) \\\\ 6w & =6 \\\\ w & =1\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=1.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-5w & =5 \\\\ 2v-5(1) & =5 \\\\ 2v-5 & =5 \\\\ 2v & =10 \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=5,\\,w=1,$ which can be expressed as $(5,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631896 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $-1,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $1.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{v}+2w & =2 \\\\ \\cancel{v}-w & =1 \\\\ (2w-w) & =(2+1) \\\\ w & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-w & =1 \\\\ v-3 & =1 \\\\ v & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=4,\\,w=3,$ which can be expressed as $(4,3).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $v$ in the first equation is $-1,$ and the coefficient of $v$ in the second equation is $1.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{v}+2w & =2 \\\\ \\cancel{v}-w & =1 \\\\ \\hline (2w-w) & =(2+1) \\\\ w & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $w=3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $w=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-w & =1 \\\\ v-3 & =1 \\\\ v & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=4,\\,w=3,$ which can be expressed as $(4,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631897 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $3$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y=8 \\\\ (\\,2x+3y=4\\,)\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y &=8 \\\\ 6x+9y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{9y} & =8 \\\\ 6x+\\cancel{9y} & =12 \\\\ (6x+6x) & =(8+12) \\\\ 12x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $12x.$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $3$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y=8 \\\\ (\\,2x+3y=4\\,)\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-9y &=8 \\\\ 6x+9y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}6x-\\cancel{9y} & =8 \\\\ 6x+\\cancel{9y} & =12 \\\\ \\hline (6x+6x) & =(8+12) \\\\ 12x & =20\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $12x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186631908 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $3,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-3.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{3y} & =12 \\\\ 9x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ (x+9x) & =(12+18) \\\\ 10x & =30 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =12 \\\\ (3)+3y & =12 \\\\ 3y & =9 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,3,3\\,).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $3,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-3.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x+\\cancel{3y} & =12 \\\\ 9x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ \\hline (x+9x) & =(12+18) \\\\ 10x & =30 \\\\ x & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =12 \\\\ (3)+3y & =12 \\\\ 3y & =9 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,3,3\\,).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631910 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $5,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-5.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+\\cancel{5y} & =20 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{5y} & =25 \\\\ (7x+2x) & =(20+25) \\\\ 9x & =45 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=5.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5y & =25 \\\\ 2(5)-5y & =25 \\\\ 10-5y & =25 \\\\ -5y & =15 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=5$ and $y=-3,$ which can be expressed as $(5,-3).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $5,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-5.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}7x+\\cancel{5y} & =20 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{5y} & =25 \\\\ \\hline (7x+2x) & =(20+25) \\\\ 9x & =45 \\\\ x & =5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=5.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5y & =25 \\\\ 2(5)-5y & =25 \\\\ 10-5y & =25 \\\\ -5y & =15 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=5$ and $y=-3,$ which can be expressed as $(5,-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631950 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3x+2y=3\\,)\\,\\times (-4) \\\\ 12x+7y=6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x-8y &=-12 \\\\ 12x+7y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{12x}-8y & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{12x}+7y & =6 \\\\ (-8y+7y) & =(-12+6) \\\\ -y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=6.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=6$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y & =3 \\\\ 3x+2(6) & =3 \\\\ 3x+12 & =3 \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=6,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,6).$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $-4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3x+2y=3\\,)\\,\\times (-4) \\\\ 12x+7y=6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x-8y &=-12 \\\\ 12x+7y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{12x}-8y & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{12x}+7y & =6 \\\\ \\hline (-8y+7y) & =(-12+6) \\\\ -y & =-6\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=6.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=6$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y & =3 \\\\ 3x+2(6) & =3 \\\\ 3x+12 & =3 \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=6,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,6).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631951 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $6,$ and the coefficient of $a$ in the second equation is $-6.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $a,$ and solve the resulting equation for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{6a}+b & =5 \\\\ \\cancel{6a}+3b & =7 \\\\ (b+3b) & =(5+7) \\\\ 4b & =12 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a+b & =5 \\\\ 6a+3 & =5 \\\\ 6a & =2 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=\\frac{1}{3},\\,b=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{3},3).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $6,$ and the coefficient of $a$ in the second equation is $-6.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $a,$ and solve the resulting equation for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{6a}+b & =5 \\\\ \\cancel{6a}+3b & =7 \\\\ \\hline (b+3b) & =(5+7) \\\\ 4b & =12 \\\\ b & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a+b & =5 \\\\ 6a+3 & =5 \\\\ 6a & =2 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=\\frac{1}{3},\\,b=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{3},3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631956 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $6,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-6.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{6y} & =-2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{6y} & =14 \\\\ (x+2x) & =(-2+14) \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=4.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+6y & =-2 \\\\ (4)+6y & =-2 \\\\ 6y & =-6 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(4,-1).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $6,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-6.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}x+\\cancel{6y} & =-2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{6y} & =14 \\\\ \\hline (x+2x) & =(-2+14) \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=4.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+6y & =-2 \\\\ (4)+6y & =-2 \\\\ 6y & =-6 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(4,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631959 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-2,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x\\cancel{-2y} & =24 \\\\ -5x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ (6x-5x) & =(24+11) \\\\ x & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=35.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=35$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-2y & =24 \\\\ 6(35)-2y & =24 \\\\ 210-2y & =24 \\\\ -2y & =-186 \\\\ y & =93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=35$ and $y=93,$ which can be expressed as $(35,93).$" "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-2,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $2.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}6x\\cancel{-2y} & =24 \\\\ -5x+\\cancel{2y} & =11 \\\\ \\hline (6x-5x) & =(24+11) \\\\ x & =35\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=35.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=35$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-2y & =24 \\\\ 6(35)-2y & =24 \\\\ 210-2y & =24 \\\\ -2y & =-186 \\\\ y & =93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=35$ and $y=93,$ which can be expressed as $(35,93).$"] [:db/cas 17592186631963 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y=1 \\\\ (\\,-2x+5y=4\\,)\\,\\times (4)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y &=1 \\\\ -8x+20y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{8x}-3y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{8x}+20y & =16 \\\\ (-3y+20y) & =(1+16) \\\\ 17y & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y=1 \\\\ (\\,-2x+5y=4\\,)\\,\\times (4)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x-3y &=1 \\\\ -8x+20y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{8x}-3y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{8x}+20y & =16 \\\\ \\hline (-3y+20y) & =(1+16) \\\\ 17y & =17\\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186631968 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4a-3b=6\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 5a+12b=-5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16a-12b &=24 \\\\ 5a+12b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16a-\\cancel{12b} & =24 \\\\ 5a+\\cancel{12b} & =-5 \\\\ (16a+5a) & =(24-5) \\\\ 21a & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $21a.$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $4$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4a-3b=6\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 5a+12b=-5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16a-12b &=24 \\\\ 5a+12b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}16a-\\cancel{12b} & =24 \\\\ 5a+\\cancel{12b} & =-5 \\\\ \\hline (16a+5a) & =(24-5) \\\\ 21a & =19\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $21a.$"] [:db/cas 17592186631970 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $7$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y=22 \\\\ (\\,4x+2y=-5\\,)\\,\\times (7)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y &=22 \\\\ 28x+14y &=-35\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-\\cancel{14y} & =22 \\\\ 28x+\\cancel{14y} & =-35 \\\\ (24x+28x) & =(22-35) \\\\ 52x & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $52x.$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $7$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y=22 \\\\ (\\,4x+2y=-5\\,)\\,\\times (7)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}24x-14y &=22 \\\\ 28x+14y &=-35\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}24x-\\cancel{14y} & =22 \\\\ 28x+\\cancel{14y} & =-35 \\\\ \\hline (24x+28x) & =(22-35) \\\\ 52x & =-13\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $52x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186631971 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4t+2v=-1\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 3t-4v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8t+4v &=-2 \\\\ 3t-4v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8t+\\cancel{4v} & =-2 \\\\ 3t-\\cancel{4v} & =1 \\\\ (8t+3t) & =(-2+1) \\\\ 11t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $11t.$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,4t+2v=-1\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 3t-4v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8t+4v &=-2 \\\\ 3t-4v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}8t+\\cancel{4v} & =-2 \\\\ 3t-\\cancel{4v} & =1 \\\\ \\hline (8t+3t) & =(-2+1) \\\\ 11t & =-1\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $11t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186632650 :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}-3 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 5 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ \\hline 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{array}\\right],\\,C=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -5\\end{array}\\right],\\,C=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c|c}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ \\hline 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186632653 :question/worked-solution "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\times 2 & 1\\times 2 \\\\ 2\\times 2 & ?\\times ?\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{22}$ must be a $2\\times 2$ block." "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ \\hline {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}1\\times 2 & 1\\times 2 \\\\ \\hline 2\\times 2 & ?\\times ?\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ \\hline ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{22}$ must be a $2\\times 2$ block."] [:db/cas 17592186632655 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{cc|c}a & b & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -3\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632663 :question/worked-solution "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\times 1 & ?\\times ? \\\\ 2\\times 1 & 2\\times 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{12}$ must be a $3\\times 2$ block." "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ \\hline {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}3\\times 1 & ?\\times ? \\\\ \\hline 2\\times 1 & 2\\times 2\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ \\hline ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{12}$ must be a $3\\times 2$ block."] [:db/cas 17592186632671 :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right],\\,A=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline -8 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right],\\,A=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c|c}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline -8 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}1 & -1 \\\\ \\hline 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}10 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186632701 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ r & 2s & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -t\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ r & 2s & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2s & -4 \\\\ 5 & -t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we have a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ r & 2s & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -t\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{c|cc}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline r & 2s & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -t\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2s & -4 \\\\ 5 & -t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632704 :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\left[\\begin{array}{c|cc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 21 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 8\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""] [:db/cas 17592186638236 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular.\nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq &=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}: &=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the $10$ million population will live in towns. Therefore, the total number of people living in towns after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{million}=2.5\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular.\nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq &=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.8 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-0.2 & 0.1 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}: &=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the $10$ million population will live in towns. Therefore, the total number of people living in towns after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 10\\,\\text{million}=2.5\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638625 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}15 & 4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186638631 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 8 & 7 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 6 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 8 & 7 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 6 & 0 & -7\\end{array}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186638643 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 8 & -7 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 6 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 8 & -7 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 6 & 0 & 7\\end{array}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186638644 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"